00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:22.554
that they're able to finally
self-document these things

00:05:22.554 --> 00:05:24.655
that they like to do and
share it with people.

00:05:24.655 --> 00:05:27.093
They're so stoked at how
good they look in the video

00:05:27.093 --> 00:05:29.590
that when they share the video,
they often give us credit.

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:30.565
My Go Pro ski trip.

00:05:30.565 --> 00:05:33.026
My Go Pro day at the park with my kids.

00:05:33.026 --> 00:05:34.802
- You're the only CEO
I've ever interviewed

00:05:34.802 --> 00:05:35.894
who's used the word stoked.

00:05:35.894 --> 00:05:36.727
- Like five million times.

00:05:36.727 --> 00:05:38.071
- [Anderson] Five million times, yes.

00:05:38.071 --> 00:05:39.139
(laughter)

00:05:39.139 --> 00:05:44.003
- Well you know, you've got
to stay true to who you are

00:05:44.003 --> 00:05:47.336
and I recognize that my approach to life

00:05:48.673 --> 00:05:52.376
and now our company's
approach to life is what

00:05:52.376 --> 00:05:53.886
has made Go Pro what it is.

00:05:53.886 --> 00:05:55.581
And so there's no reason to change that.

00:05:55.581 --> 00:05:57.020
- You have to be authentic.

00:05:57.020 --> 00:05:59.958
I mean, you've got no
hope if you try to pretend

00:05:59.958 --> 00:06:03.116
to be Reed Hastings or Mark Zuckerberg

00:06:03.116 --> 00:06:05.031
or Bill Gates or whoever you want to think

00:06:05.031 --> 00:06:07.700
your CEOs are that you've met.

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:09.766
You know, that is crazy.

00:06:09.766 --> 00:06:12.169
And you've got to really get
comfortable with yourself,

00:06:12.169 --> 00:06:14.259
your strengths and weaknesses.

00:06:14.259 --> 00:06:17.176
People are inspired by authenticity

00:06:18.137 --> 00:06:22.073
and I'm just surprised how many people

00:06:22.073 --> 00:06:26.101
feel like they're acting or try to act.

00:06:26.101 --> 00:06:29.712
And you may be a really
quiet person and that's fine.

00:06:29.712 --> 00:06:33.625
But if you have that
fierce will, that desire

00:06:33.625 --> 00:06:37.851
for the institution,
the company to be great,

00:06:37.851 --> 00:06:40.498
and your own role in that is secondary,

00:06:40.498 --> 00:06:42.704
and you authentically
express that in whatever ways

00:06:42.704 --> 00:06:46.535
you do, then you'll be greatly accepted.

00:06:46.535 --> 00:06:49.774
Okay, and so that's all it
takes, is to be authentic

00:06:49.774 --> 00:06:52.828
and not try to be something
else, and to fundamentally

00:06:52.828 --> 00:06:56.612
care at your core about
what's good for the company.

00:06:56.612 --> 00:06:59.352
And if everybody knows,
and you act in the notion

00:06:59.352 --> 00:07:01.953
of what's good as best as
your judgment can tell,

00:07:01.953 --> 00:07:04.414
what's good for the
company, they will see that.

00:07:04.414 --> 00:07:05.691
And people will follow you.

00:07:05.691 --> 00:07:06.957
And they won't be cynical.

00:07:06.957 --> 00:07:09.745
It's when you hide things,
it's when you pretend,

00:07:09.745 --> 00:07:11.888
that you lose trust very quickly.

00:07:11.888 --> 00:07:14.059
- Man you just got to stay
real to yourself, you know,

00:07:14.059 --> 00:07:15.661
not ever try to follow the trends.

00:07:15.661 --> 00:07:17.263
That's when you get
lost in the sauce, man,

00:07:17.263 --> 00:07:20.839
you got to do what you feel
man, and be creative in it.

00:07:20.839 --> 00:07:23.173
Don't be scared to be yourself,
always keep that faith.

00:07:23.173 --> 00:07:25.611
I've been in the darkest times of my life,

00:07:25.611 --> 00:07:27.248
when I didn't know what
was going to happen,

00:07:27.248 --> 00:07:29.011
and I was flat broke.

00:07:29.011 --> 00:07:31.878
Anything you can think of
man, I done been through it.

00:07:31.878 --> 00:07:34.885
You got to be strong
enough to make it through.

00:07:34.885 --> 00:07:36.371
And it just makes it that much sweeter,

00:07:36.371 --> 00:07:38.124
when everything starts going right.

00:07:38.124 --> 00:07:39.657
- [Narrator] When she was 17 years old,

00:07:39.657 --> 00:07:41.816
Stephanie got her high school
diploma and left the nuns

00:07:41.816 --> 00:07:44.963
to join the prestigious
Tisch School of Arts,

00:07:44.963 --> 00:07:47.854
a school entirely dedicated
to artistic disciplines.

00:07:47.854 --> 00:07:49.827
She took numerous classes
such as art story,

00:07:49.827 --> 00:07:51.696
music, theater and dancing.

00:07:51.696 --> 00:07:54.135
- I was really blessed
and lucky to get in.

00:07:54.135 --> 00:07:55.802
I got in very early.

00:07:56.770 --> 00:08:00.687
I was 17 and I was one
of 20 people in America

00:08:02.343 --> 00:08:04.926
that got into the school early.

00:08:06.313 --> 00:08:09.146
And I was really happy and
excited and I just felt

00:08:09.146 --> 00:08:10.783
